f $u+v?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410992 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c3ce546-4615-54b8-9d08-ac0c684680c7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410992 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}55 & = & 24\\cdot 2 & + & 7 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 24 & = & 7\\cdot 3 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 7 & = & 3\\cdot 2 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{gcd}(55,24)=1.$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}7 & =55-24\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & =24-7\\cdot 3 \\\\ 1 & =7-3\\cdot 2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =7-3\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =7-[24-7\\cdot 3]\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =7\\cdot 7-24\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =[55-24\\cdot 2]\\cdot 7-24\\cdot 2 \\\\  & =55\\cdot 7-24\\cdot 16 \\\\  & =55\\cdot 7+24\\cdot (-16)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=7$ and $v=-16.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nu+v=7+(-16)=-9.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410993 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410989 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410993 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103236" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410993 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410993 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $80u+35v=\\text{gcd}(80,35).$ What is the value of $u+v?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410993 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "321cfd56-0a7b-5b4f-978f-55f6493e6553" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410993 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}80 & = & 35\\cdot 2 & + & 10 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 35 & = & 10\\cdot 3 & + & 5 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 10 & = & 5\\cdot 2 & + & 0\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAs a result, $\\text{gcd}(80,35)=5.$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}10 & =80-35\\cdot 2 \\\\ 5 & =35-10\\cdot 3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}5 & =35-10\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =35-[80-35\\cdot 2]\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =35\\cdot 7-80\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =80\\cdot (-3)+35\\cdot 7\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=-3$ and $v=7.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nu+v=(-3)+7=4.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410994 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410988 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410994 #edb/ref 1069 "q-103258" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410994 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410994 #edb/ref 1071 "Using the extended Euclidean algorithm, compute integers $u$ and $v$ such that $71u+29v=\\text{gcd}(71,29).$ What is the value of $u+v?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410994 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "32ff23bf-0d8e-550e-a2d3-333ad056d2c6" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410994 #edb/ref 1074 "We will use the extended Euclidean algorithm. First, we apply the forward reduction:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{aligned}71 & = & 29\\cdot 2 & + & 13 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 29 & = & 13\\cdot 2 & + & 3 \\\\  & ↙ &  & ↙ &  \\\\ 13 & = & 3\\cdot 4 & + & 1\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n  \n\nAs a result, $\\text{gcd}(71,29)=1.$\n\nSolving for the remainders (the rightmost terms) in the equations above, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}13 & =71-29\\cdot 2 \\\\ 3 & =29-13\\cdot 2 \\\\ 1 & =13-3\\cdot 4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, we back-substitute:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}1 & =13-3\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =13-[29-13\\cdot 2]\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =13\\cdot 9-29\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =[71-29\\cdot 2]\\cdot 9-29\\cdot 4 \\\\  & =71\\cdot 9-29\\cdot 22 \\\\  & =71\\cdot 9+29\\cdot (-22)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $u=9$ and $v=-22.$\n\nFinally, \n\n$$\nu+v=9+(-22)=-13.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410995 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186410984 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410995 #edb/ref 1069 "q-79902" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186410995 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/r